---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import TrustBadge from "../components/TrustBadge.astro";
import SocialProof from "../components/SocialProof.astro";

const title = "Treadmill Belt Size Guide (2026): Deck Length and Width by Height";
const description =
  "Deck length is set by your stride, not your height alone, and 55 inches is the line where taller runners start clipping the motor hood. The sizes that work, by user and by pace.";

const SIZES = [
  {
    user: "Under 5'3\", walking only",
    size: "48 x 18 in",
    verdict: "Compact decks are genuinely fine here. Do not overpay for length.",
  },
  {
    user: "5'3\" – 5'10\", walking only",
    size: "50 – 55 x 18 in",
    verdict: "Comfortable. Width matters more than length at walking speeds.",
  },
  {
    user: "Under 5'10\", jogging",
    size: "55 x 20 in",
    verdict: "The mainstream home size, and adequate for most joggers.",
  },
  {
    user: "5'10\" – 6'2\", jogging or running",
    size: "60 x 20 in",
    verdict: "The point where 55 in starts feeling short on the back stride.",
  },
  {
    user: "Over 6'2\", any running",
    size: "60 – 62 x 22 in",
    verdict: "Non-negotiable. A short deck forces a shortened, unnatural gait.",
  },
  {
    user: "Sprint or interval training",
    size: "60 x 22 in minimum",
    verdict: "Stride lengthens sharply with pace. Width buys recovery room.",
  },
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 1, name: "Home", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/" },
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 2, name: "Buying Guides", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/best-of/" },
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 3, name: "Treadmill Belt Size Guide", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/treadmill-belt-size-guide/" },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What size treadmill belt do I need?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "For walking, 50 to 55 inches long by 18 inches wide is enough for most people. For jogging under 5 foot 10, buy 55 by 20 inches. For running, or for anyone over 5 foot 10, buy 60 by 20 inches, and over 6 foot 2 look for 60 by 22. Length follows your stride, and stride grows faster with pace than with height.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Is a 55 inch treadmill belt long enough for running?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "It is long enough for a runner under about 5 foot 10 at moderate pace, and short for anyone taller or faster. The symptom of a deck that is too short is not falling off the back — it is unconsciously shortening your stride to stay on it, which changes your gait and is the reason some people find treadmill running uncomfortable in a way outdoor running is not.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Does treadmill belt width matter?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "It matters more than most buyers expect, because it determines your margin for lateral error. An 18-inch belt is fine for walking, where your feet land in a predictable line. At running pace, and particularly when tired, foot placement wanders, and 20 to 22 inches is what keeps a slightly wide step on the belt rather than on the side rail.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How do I measure my treadmill belt for a replacement?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Measure the belt itself rather than the deck. With the machine unplugged, mark a point on the belt edge, run a tape along the full loop until you return to the mark, and measure the width across the belt. Replacement belts are sold by loop length and width, and a belt sized to the deck instead of the loop will not fit.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Is a bigger treadmill belt always better?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "No. A larger deck adds footprint, weight and cost, and needs a stronger motor to move the extra belt mass. For a household that walks, a 60-inch deck is a large object bought for a benefit nobody in the house will use. Buy the deck your longest stride needs and put the remaining budget into motor headroom and cushioning.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What is the standard treadmill belt size?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "The most common home size is 55 by 20 inches, which is the default in the 800 to 1,500 dollar band. Budget and folding machines usually run 50 by 18 inches or smaller, and machines above about 1,500 dollars typically move to 60 by 20. Commercial gym treadmills are usually 60 by 22.",
          },
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      description: description,
      author: { "@type": "Person", name: "Morgan Reyes" },
      datePublished: "2026-09-06",
      dateModified: "2026-09-06",
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "Treadmill Reviews USA" },
    },
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={`${title} | Treadmill Reviews USA`}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: "2026-09-06",
    modifiedTime: "2026-09-06",
    author: "Morgan Reyes",
    section: "Fitness Equipment",
    tags: [
      "treadmill belt size",
      "treadmill deck length",
      "treadmill running surface",
      "treadmill buying guide",
      "home gym",
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
      <svg class="absolute inset-0 w-full h-full opacity-10" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
        <pattern id="dot-pattern-size" x="0" y="0" width="24" height="24" patternUnits="userSpaceOnUse">
          <circle cx="2" cy="2" r="1.5" fill="white"></circle>
        </pattern>
        <rect width="100%" height="100%" fill="url(#dot-pattern-size)"></rect>
      </svg>
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6">
          <TrustBadge />
        </div>
        <div class="flex items-center justify-center gap-3 mb-6 flex-wrap">
          <span class="bg-[#FF5A1F] text-white px-3 py-1 rounded-full text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest">
            Buyer's Guide
          </span>
          <span class="bg-green-500 text-white px-3 py-1 rounded-full text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest">
            Updated September 2026
          </span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight mb-8">
          Treadmill <span class="text-[#5AA9FF]">Belt Size</span> Guide
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 max-w-2xl mx-auto leading-relaxed">
          Deck length is the specification people notice last and regret first.
          It is set by your stride rather than your height, and the difference
          between 55 and 60 inches decides whether treadmill running feels
          natural or slightly wrong in a way you cannot name.
        </p>
        <div class="mt-8 flex flex-wrap justify-center gap-4">
          <a href="#sizes" class="bg-[#FF5A1F] hover:bg-[#E64F16] text-white px-8 py-4 rounded-xl font-black no-underline transition-colors uppercase tracking-widest text-sm">
            Find Your Size
          </a>
          <a href="#measuring" class="bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white px-8 py-4 rounded-xl font-black no-underline transition-colors uppercase tracking-widest text-sm border border-white/20">
            Measuring a Replacement
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-4 py-8 border-b border-gray-100">
        <img src="https://i.pravatar.cc/150?img=48" alt="Morgan Reyes" class="w-12 h-12 rounded-full" />
        <div>
          <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 leading-tight">Morgan Reyes</p>
          <p class="text-gray-500 text-sm leading-tight">Fitness Equipment Editor</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block w-px h-8 bg-gray-200"></div>
        <p class="text-gray-500 text-sm">
          Last Updated: <strong class="text-gray-700">September 6, 2026</strong>
        </p>
        <div class="ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="4.7k" />
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="not-prose my-10 bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
        <p class="text-sm text-gray-700 m-0 leading-relaxed">
          <strong class="text-gray-900">The short answer.</strong> Walkers are fine
          on 50 by 18 inches. Joggers under 5 foot 10 want 55 by 20. Runners, and
          anyone over 5 foot 10, want 60 by 20. Over 6 foot 2, or doing intervals,
          want 60 by 22. Buying more deck than your stride needs costs floor space
          and motor load for nothing.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
        <h2 class="flex items-center gap-2 text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mt-0 mb-4">
          <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="w-5 h-5 text-[#FF5A1F]" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M4 6h16M4 12h16M4 18h7"></path></svg>
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#why-stride" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">1. Why Stride Beats Height</a>
          <a href="#sizes" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">2. The Size Table</a>
          <a href="#length" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">3. What Deck Length Actually Buys</a>
          <a href="#width" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">4. Width Is the Underrated Number</a>
          <a href="#too-short" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">5. How a Short Deck Feels</a>
          <a href="#tradeoffs" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">6. What a Bigger Deck Costs You</a>
          <a href="#measuring" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">7. Measuring for a Replacement Belt</a>
          <a href="#thickness" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">8. Belt Thickness and Ply</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">9. Frequently Asked Questions</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Nobody returns a treadmill because the belt is an inch too narrow. They
          keep it, use it less than they intended, and never quite work out why
          running on it feels like a compromise. Deck size is the specification
          most responsible for that quiet disappointment, and it is completely
          predictable before you buy.
        </p>

        <div id="why-stride" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">1. Why Stride Beats Height</h2>
          <p>
            Every belt size chart on the internet indexes on height, and height is
            only a proxy. What the deck has to accommodate is the distance between
            your footfalls, and that distance grows with pace far more sharply
            than it grows with leg length.
          </p>
          <p>
            A 5 foot 8 walker moving at 3 mph puts down roughly 26 to 28 inches
            between heel strikes. The same person at a 9-minute mile is closer to
            48 inches, and at a sprint might exceed 60. Height changes the
            baseline by a few inches. Pace changes it by a factor of two. That is
            why the size table below is organised by what you will do on the
            machine first and how tall you are second.
          </p>
          <p>
            You also need more deck than your stride length, not exactly your
            stride length. Your foot lands somewhere in the front half of the
            belt, travels backward with it, and lifts before the rear roller. The
            usable running surface is roughly the belt length minus the four to
            six inches at the front where the motor hood intrudes and the few
            inches at the back you would not want to be standing on. A 55-inch
            belt gives a genuine working surface of something closer to 46.
          </p>
          <p>
            The practical method: take your normal training pace, not your fastest
            or your slowest. If you can honestly say you will walk on this
            machine, buy for walking and spend the saving elsewhere. If you intend
            to run and are buying a walking deck because it fits the room, be
            aware you are buying a walking machine, and consider whether a
            <a href="/folding-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">folding machine</a>
            with a full-size deck solves the space problem better.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="sizes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">2. The Size Table</h2>
          <p>
            Sizes are belt dimensions, not deck or frame dimensions, which is how
            manufacturers quote them. Length first, width second, as the industry
            writes it.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="not-prose my-8 overflow-x-auto">
        <table class="min-w-[680px] w-full text-sm border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden">
          <thead class="bg-gray-900 text-white">
            <tr>
              <th class="text-left px-4 py-3 font-black">User and Use</th>
              <th class="text-left px-4 py-3 font-black">Belt Size</th>
              <th class="text-left px-4 py-3 font-black">Notes</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {
              SIZES.map((row, i) => (
                <tr class={i % 2 === 0 ? "bg-white" : "bg-gray-50"}>
                  <td class="px-4 py-3 font-bold text-gray-900 align-top">{row.user}</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700 align-top">{row.size}</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-500 align-top">{row.verdict}</td>
                </tr>
              ))
            }
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p>
          Where a household spans two rows, buy for the taller and faster user.
          The person who needs less deck is not inconvenienced by having more of
          it, and the person who needs more is genuinely constrained by having
          less. This is the one specification where the shared-machine compromise
          runs entirely one way.
        </p>

        <div id="length" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. What Deck Length Actually Buys</h2>
          <p>
            Length does one thing: it gives your foot somewhere to land that is
            not the motor hood at the front or thin air at the back. Everything
            else people attribute to deck length is really about the deck
            construction underneath.
          </p>
          <p>
            The front matters more than the back, which is counter-intuitive. Most
            people picture falling off the rear, but the constraint that actually
            bites is how close your leading foot comes to the console shroud as
            your stride opens up. Runners on short decks report the sensation of
            running slightly uphill into an obstacle, and they compensate by
            moving backward on the belt, which puts them nearer the rear roller
            and makes the machine feel unstable.
          </p>
          <p>
            Fifty-five inches is the industry's compromise and it is a reasonable
            one for a lot of people. It is also exactly the point at which a
            6-foot runner's stride stops fitting comfortably. If you are between
            5 foot 10 and 6 foot and you genuinely run, 60 inches is the upgrade
            worth paying for even at the cost of a smaller screen or fewer
            programmes.
          </p>
          <p>
            Above 60 inches, returns fall off quickly for home use. Sixty-two and
            63-inch decks exist and are useful for very tall runners and for
            sprint work, but the extra length adds footprint and belt mass for a
            narrow benefit. It is not the place to spend a marginal 300 dollars
            unless you are over about 6 foot 4.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="width" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. Width Is the Underrated Number</h2>
          <p>
            Length gets all the attention and width is the one people notice
            underfoot. It governs your margin for lateral error, and that margin
            is what determines whether you can relax on the machine.
          </p>
          <p>
            At walking pace, feet land in a narrow, repeatable line and 18 inches
            is genuinely enough. This is why walking pads and
            <a href="/under-desk-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">under-desk treadmills</a>
            can be narrow without being unpleasant. At running pace, and
            especially in the last third of a session when form degrades, foot
            placement wanders by an inch or two either side. On an 18-inch belt
            that is a clipped side rail. On a 20-inch belt it is nothing.
          </p>
          <p>
            The other thing width buys is the ability to step off safely. Straddling
            the belt onto the side rails to pause is a routine manoeuvre, and the
            rails on a narrow machine are correspondingly narrow. Anyone with
            balance concerns should treat width as a safety specification, which is
            part of why it features in our
            <a href="/best-treadmills-for-seniors/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">senior treadmill scoring</a>.
          </p>
          <p>
            Twenty-two inches is a genuine step up in feel for runners and is
            standard on commercial machines, but it comes with a real cost: the
            machine gets wider by more than two inches once you account for rails
            and frame, and belt mass rises, which asks more of the motor. It is
            worth it for tall runners and interval work, and unnecessary for
            steady-state jogging.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="too-short" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. How a Short Deck Feels</h2>
          <p>
            The failure mode of an undersized deck is not dramatic. Almost nobody
            runs off the back of a treadmill. What happens instead is that you
            adapt, unconsciously and immediately, and the adaptation is the
            problem.
          </p>
          <p>
            The most common adaptation is stride shortening with a higher cadence.
            You take smaller steps more often to keep the same speed on a surface
            that does not have room for your natural step. That changes the
            mechanics of the movement — more time on the forefoot, less hip
            extension — and it is why some people find that treadmill running
            aggravates a calf or hip niggle that outdoor running never touches.
          </p>
          <p>
            The second adaptation is drifting rearward. Runners who feel crowded at
            the front settle further back on the belt, where the deck is often less
            supported and the rear roller is close. It is the main reason people
            describe a machine as feeling flimsy at pace when the frame is
            actually fine.
          </p>
          <p>
            The third is simply using it less. This is the one that costs the most
            money, and it is invisible because nobody attributes it to a
            specification. A machine that is slightly wrong for you gets used
            twice a week instead of five times, and no amount of console features
            fixes it.
          </p>
          <p>
            If you already own a machine that feels short, there is no
            modification that adds deck. What you can do is make sure the deck you
            have performs properly — a well-lubricated deck feels meaningfully
            better underfoot than a dry one, which our
            <a href="/treadmill-belt-lubrication/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">lubrication guide</a>
            covers — and reserve faster work for outdoors.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="tradeoffs" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. What a Bigger Deck Costs You</h2>
          <p>
            The honest other side. Deck size is not free, and the reflex to buy the
            biggest one available is as wasteful as buying too small is
            frustrating.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Footprint.</strong> A 60-inch deck typically means a machine
            around 80 inches long overall, against roughly 70 for a 50-inch deck.
            In a spare room that difference is trivial. In a bedroom or a
            hallway-adjacent space it decides whether the machine fits at all, and
            it changes the safety clearance you need behind it — see
            <a href="/treadmill-dimensions-space-requirements/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">dimensions and space requirements</a>
            for the numbers that include clearance.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Weight.</strong> Bigger decks mean heavier machines, typically
            30 to 60 lb more. That is good for stability and bad for anyone who
            needs to move the machine, get it upstairs, or fold it away daily. A
            folding machine with a 60-inch deck is a heavy object to lift even
            with a hydraulic assist.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Motor load.</strong> A longer, wider belt is more mass to keep
            moving and more surface area in contact with the deck, so it draws
            more from the motor at the same speed. A large deck on a modest motor
            is a genuinely bad combination, and it is common in the folding
            category where the marketing sells deck size. Pair deck size with the
            motor advice in our
            <a href="/treadmill-horsepower-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">horsepower guide</a>
            rather than treating them as separate decisions.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Money.</strong> Roughly speaking, moving from 55 to 60 inches
            within a manufacturer's range costs a few hundred dollars, and that
            money could otherwise buy a better motor or better cushioning. For a
            household of walkers, it is the wrong place to spend it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="measuring" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. Measuring for a Replacement Belt</h2>
          <p>
            If you are here because a belt has worn out rather than because you are
            shopping, the numbers you need are different. Replacement belts are
            sold by the dimensions of the loop, not the dimensions of the deck,
            and the two are not the same.
          </p>
          <p>
            With the machine unplugged, mark a point on the belt edge with a piece
            of tape. Run a flexible tape measure along the belt, keeping it on the
            surface, and advance the belt by hand until your mark comes back
            around. That total is the loop length, and it will be noticeably more
            than twice the deck length because the belt wraps both rollers. Then
            measure straight across the belt for the width.
          </p>
          <p>
            Check the belt itself for a printed part number before you measure
            anything — many manufacturers print one on the underside near the
            seam, and ordering by part number removes the guesswork entirely. The
            model number of the machine, on the sticker at the front of the frame
            or under the hood, is the next best route.
          </p>
          <p>
            One thing worth deciding at the same time: inspect the deck board while
            the belt is off. A deck with a visible groove worn along the running
            line, or a surface that has gone rough, will destroy a new belt within
            months. Many decks are reversible and can be flipped to an unused face,
            which is cheap and effective. Fitting a new belt to a worn deck is
            money spent twice.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="thickness" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. Belt Thickness and Ply</h2>
          <p>
            Length and width are the numbers on the box. Thickness and construction
            are the ones that decide how the belt lasts, and they are rarely
            advertised at all on home machines.
          </p>
          <p>
            Belts are described by <strong>ply</strong> — the number of bonded
            layers. A one-ply belt is thin, light and easy for a small motor to
            move, and it wears through faster. A two-ply belt has a fabric backing
            bonded to the running surface, lasts substantially longer, feels more
            solid underfoot and asks more of the motor. Commercial machines
            sometimes use three or four ply, which is a durability choice for
            fourteen-hour days rather than anything you would feel.
          </p>
          <p>
            For a home machine that will be run daily, two-ply is worth having and
            worth asking about, because it is the difference between replacing the
            belt in year three and replacing it in year seven. Where a
            manufacturer does not publish it, machine weight and price band are
            reasonable proxies: budget folding machines are almost universally
            one-ply.
          </p>
          <p>
            Thicker is not automatically better on an underpowered machine. A
            two-ply belt on a 2.0 CHP motor adds drag the motor is not sized for,
            and the manufacturer specified one-ply for a reason. When replacing,
            match what came out unless you are also confident the motor has
            headroom to spare.
          </p>
          <p>
            Whatever the construction, the thing that determines belt life is not
            the belt. It is whether the deck beneath it has lubricant on it. A
            one-ply belt on a lubricated deck outlasts a two-ply belt on a dry one
            by a wide margin, which is the argument our
            <a href="/treadmill-maintenance/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">maintenance schedule</a>
            makes at length.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">9. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What size treadmill belt do I need?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For walking, 50 to 55 inches long by 18 inches wide is enough for
                most people. For jogging under 5 foot 10, buy 55 by 20 inches. For
                running, or for anyone over 5 foot 10, buy 60 by 20 inches, and
                over 6 foot 2 look for 60 by 22. Length follows your stride, and
                stride grows faster with pace than with height.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a 55 inch treadmill belt long enough for running?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It is long enough for a runner under about 5 foot 10 at moderate
                pace, and short for anyone taller or faster. The symptom of a deck
                that is too short is not falling off the back — it is
                unconsciously shortening your stride to stay on it, which changes
                your gait and is the reason some people find treadmill running
                uncomfortable in a way outdoor running is not.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does treadmill belt width matter?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It matters more than most buyers expect, because it determines your
                margin for lateral error. An 18-inch belt is fine for walking,
                where your feet land in a predictable line. At running pace, and
                particularly when tired, foot placement wanders, and 20 to 22
                inches is what keeps a slightly wide step on the belt rather than
                on the side rail.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I measure my treadmill belt for a replacement?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Measure the belt itself rather than the deck. With the machine
                unplugged, mark a point on the belt edge, run a tape along the full
                loop until you return to the mark, and measure the width across the
                belt. Replacement belts are sold by loop length and width, and a
                belt sized to the deck instead of the loop will not fit.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a bigger treadmill belt always better?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. A larger deck adds footprint, weight and cost, and needs a
                stronger motor to move the extra belt mass. For a household that
                walks, a 60-inch deck is a large object bought for a benefit nobody
                in the house will use. Buy the deck your longest stride needs and
                put the remaining budget into motor headroom and cushioning.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the standard treadmill belt size?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The most common home size is 55 by 20 inches, which is the default
                in the 800 to 1,500 dollar band. Budget and folding machines
                usually run 50 by 18 inches or smaller, and machines above about
                1,500 dollars typically move to 60 by 20. Commercial gym treadmills
                are usually 60 by 22.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes Choosing a Belt Size</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Deck size is hard to change after purchase, which makes these four expensive.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sizing on height instead of pace</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Height moves your stride by a few inches; pace can double it. A 5 foot 6 runner needs more deck than a 6 foot walker, and every height-indexed chart gets that backwards. Decide what you will actually do on the machine first, then adjust for height.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a short deck to fit the room</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A running deck that does not fit is a real constraint, but a walking deck bought by a runner is a machine that gets used twice a week. Before compromising on deck length, check whether a folding machine with a full-size deck solves the space problem, because most of the footprint is recovered when it folds.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Pairing a large deck with a small motor</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A 60 by 22 inch belt is significantly more mass and contact area than a 50 by 18, and it draws more from the motor at every speed. Budget folding machines advertise deck size precisely because it is cheap to add. Check the continuous horsepower rating before being impressed by the dimensions.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ordering a replacement belt by deck length</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Belts are sold by loop length, which is more than twice the deck length because the belt wraps both rollers. Measure the full loop by marking the belt and walking a tape around it, or better, order by the part number printed on the underside. A belt ordered to deck dimensions will not fit at all.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Related Guides -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">Related Buying Guides</h2>
          <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-3 gap-4">
